## 1. Problem Statement

The Photonic Rendering Pipeline architecture proposes that a photonic rendering chiplet receives input data (G-buffer, motion vectors, neural features) from an electronic GPU at rates of **30–108 GB/s** and returns a processed optical image representation.

**The central question of this task:**

> At what combination of bandwidth, distance, and technology maturity does an optical (photonic) interconnect between an electronic compute chiplet and a photonic rendering chiplet become more energy-efficient than an equivalent electrical interconnect (e.g., UCIe)?

If the answer is "never" for the target bandwidth and distance range, that is a valid and important result — it would mean the input interface to the photonic chiplet should remain electrical, and only the output (chiplet → display) should be optical.

---

## 2. Background

### 2.1 Why this matters

The parent document (Section 5, "Input Data and the E/O Boundary") explicitly identifies input data volume as a critical issue:

- Input traffic to the photonic chiplet: **30–108 GB/s**
- Display-link traffic it would replace: **~3.7 GB/s** (4K120 10-bit video)
- Ratio: input traffic is **8–30× larger** than the output traffic

This means the photonic chiplet's input interface is the most bandwidth-demanding link in the entire proposed architecture. If this interface is not energy-efficient, the architectural advantage of the photonic pipeline may be negated at the system level.

### 2.2 Two competing interconnect paradigms

| Parameter | Electrical (UCIe) | Optical (photonic) |
|-----------|-------------------|-------------------|
| Energy per bit (shipping) | 0.25–0.5 pJ/bit | 3–5 pJ/bit |
| Energy per bit (research) | ~0.25 pJ/bit (near theoretical limit) | 0.013–0.8 pJ/bit (modulator only) |
| Bandwidth density | High (Tbit/s/mm²) | High (WDM, but area overhead for lasers/couplers) |
| Distance scaling | Degrades with distance (RC, skin effect) | Low loss over distance |
| Maturity | Standard (UCIe 1.0, 2022) | Early commercial (Ayar Labs, Intel OCI) |

The key tension: **electrical interconnect is currently 6–20× more energy-efficient per bit at short distances (mm–cm), but optical interconnect may scale better at higher bandwidth or longer distances.**

